        body: 'Equipment quality is the most visible criterion but not always the most important. A gym with cutting-edge machines but no knowledgeable staff is less valuable than a well-maintained facility with coaches who can actually teach you to use equipment correctly and safely. Look for: certified trainers (not just "gym instructors"), clean and well-maintained equipment, adequate spacing between machines, good ventilation and lighting, and a membership structure that doesn\'t lock you into long contracts you can\'t exit.\n\nThe member community matters too. A gym where the regulars are serious about their training tends to create an environment where you work harder. If the ambience feels casual and social (which is fine for some people), understand that this will influence your training intensity.',
